# Environment and measured checkout revision for the U4 local timing series.
# Neither host is a GitHub runner.

== Measured revision

Both hosts ran the source of commit e76e235b, the merge the U4 branch started
from. That branch's only later commit, 68e4f77b, changes PLAN-GATE.md and
docs/gate-cost-baseline.md and no source:

$ git diff --stat e76e235b 68e4f77b
 PLAN-GATE.md               | 127 ++++++++++++++++++-----------
 docs/gate-cost-baseline.md | 197 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 275 insertions(+), 49 deletions(-)

The macOS host ran a throwaway clone rsynced from the Linux worktree, without
.git, so its revision is established by content. Of the 130 tracked files that
the harness object key covers, 129 are byte-identical in the two trees and only
tools/test262.ts differs, by the preserved pool patch:

$ git ls-files -- aube-lock.yaml mise.toml tools/test262.ts \
    packages/compiler/src packages/compiler/package.json \
    packages/backend-c/src packages/backend-c/package.json \
    packages/parser-babel/src packages/parser-babel/package.json \
    packages/cli/src packages/cli/package.json \
    packages/unicode/src packages/unicode/package.json \
    packages/runtime-c tests/test262/harness \
  | LC_ALL=C sort > /tmp/keyfiles.txt
$ wc -l < /tmp/keyfiles.txt
130
$ xargs -a /tmp/keyfiles.txt sha256sum | LC_ALL=C sort -k2 > /tmp/keys-linux.txt
$ scp /tmp/keyfiles.txt mac-mini-m4:/tmp/keyfiles.txt
$ ssh mac-mini-m4 'cd ~/Desktop/oseo-m5ci \
    && xargs shasum -a 256 < /tmp/keyfiles.txt | LC_ALL=C sort -k2' \
  > /tmp/keys-mac.txt
$ diff /tmp/keys-linux.txt /tmp/keys-mac.txt \
    | grep '^[<>]' | awk '{print $NF}' | sort | uniq -c
      2 tools/test262.ts

$ sha256sum tools/test262.ts                     # e76e235b, unpatched
e521917358499c3bdaf5943c55df58807a4ca414c7a084745b308b9eb2e9a48d
$ ssh mac-mini-m4 'shasum -a 256 ~/Desktop/oseo-m5ci/tools/test262.ts'
b81f4eea567660174e25d50d3192a0e074cea8d2deedc7ca3cafb817ab10ee6f

Applying pool3.patch.txt to e76e235b's tools/test262.ts reproduces the second
digest, so the macOS pool=3 runs carry exactly that one edit:

$ mkdir -p /tmp/u4-patch/tools
$ git show e76e235b:tools/test262.ts > /tmp/u4-patch/tools/test262.ts
$ git -C /tmp/u4-patch apply -p1 "$PWD/docs/evidence/u4/pool3.patch.txt"
$ sha256sum /tmp/u4-patch/tools/test262.ts
b81f4eea567660174e25d50d3192a0e074cea8d2deedc7ca3cafb817ab10ee6f

These three comparisons were run during this preservation pass, on the same
two hosts, against the trees the measurement left in place. The digests are
properties of those retained trees, not new timings.

== Linux host

$ grep -m1 'model name' /proc/cpuinfo
model name	: AMD Ryzen 7 7700X 8-Core Processor
$ nproc
16
$ uname -srm
Linux 7.2.7-200.fc44.x86_64 x86_64
$ node -e 'console.log(require("node:os").availableParallelism())'
16

availableParallelism is 16 here, so the pool=8 rows are the runner's own
min(8, availableParallelism()) cap rather than the host's width, and the
pool=3 rows come from taskset -c 0-2. Every log line records the pool the run
actually used. mise.toml pins zig = "0.16.0" at this revision, and
ZIG_GLOBAL_CACHE_DIR was /data/zig-cache/m5ci-harness-object-cache throughout.
XDG_CACHE_HOME pointed at the session scratchpad, so the harness object
directory under test was $XDG_CACHE_HOME/oseo/harness-objects.

== macOS host

Observed before the series, 2026-09-27T21:05+09:00:

$ ssh mac-mini-m4 'sysctl -n machdep.cpu.brand_string; sysctl -n hw.ncpu; \
    sw_vers -productVersion; df -h ~ | tail -1'
Apple M4
10
15.2
/dev/disk3s5   228Gi   178Gi    13Gi    93%    514k  141M    0%   /System/Volumes/Data

Observed during the pool=8 series, 2026-09-27T22:10+09:00:

$ ssh mac-mini-m4 'echo "loadavg: $(sysctl -n vm.loadavg)"; df -h ~ | tail -1'
loadavg: { 7.10 8.19 8.28 }
/dev/disk3s5   228Gi   177Gi    13Gi    94%    723k  134M    1%   /System/Volumes/Data

The baseline's "94 percent full" is the second of these. The macOS harness
object directory is ~/Library/Caches/oseo/harness-objects, which
XDG_CACHE_HOME does not redirect. Both timestamps are the originating tool
call's own, converted to +09:00.
